    "response": "An all-male selection panel—especially when evaluating candidates for a senior leadership role where a less-experienced male candidate is selected over a more-experienced female candidate—is a valid reason to scrutinize the process. \n\nHere is an objective breakdown of what this composition might signify, how it affects your situation, and how to approach next steps.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Is it a sign of anything?\n\n#### **A. Risk of Affinity Bias (Homophily)**\nWhile an all-male panel is not definitive proof of deliberate discrimination, it creates a well-documented vulnerability to **unconscious or affinity bias** (\"like hires like\"):\n* **Subjective vs. Objective Metrics:** You possess nearly double the years of design experience, a proven record of leading major projects, and identical core credentials (chartered status). The reason highlighted for the selected candidate—*\"excellent client relationships\"*—is inherently subjective. Subjective criteria are the most common vehicles for unconscious bias, where rapport and comfort during an interview are conflated with competence.\n* **Industry Demographics vs. Modern Best Practice:** Structural engineering has historically had low representation of senior women, making all-male boards common. However, modern corporate governance and HR best practice strongly advocate for diverse interview panels specifically to mitigate groupthink and biased assessments.\n\n#### **B. The Two-Week Silence**\nA two-week delay in providing written feedback is frustrating and poor practice, but not necessarily a sign of bad faith on its own. Delays often stem from HR bureaucracy, panel members being occupied with billable project work, or legal/HR teams reviewing feedback notes before release.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should you make something of it?\n\nHow and whether to raise the panel's gender composition depends on your primary goal:\n\n| Objective | Considerations |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Obtaining candid, constructive feedback** | Raising the panel’s gender composition right now will almost certainly cause HR and leadership to adopt a defensive, risk-mitigation posture, likely resulting in generic, sanitized responses prepared by legal/HR. |\n| **Preserving industry / client relationships** | Direct accusations without concrete evidence can lead to professional friction in a close-knit engineering market. |\n| **Challenging the decision / formal grievance** | In most legal jurisdictions, an all-male panel and an outcome favoring a male candidate are **circumstantial context**, not standalone proof of unlawful discrimination. You would typically need evidence of inconsistent scoring, shifting job criteria, or improper questioning. |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recommended Next Steps\n\n#### **Step 1: Send a Focused, Professional Follow-up for Feedback**\nSend a polite reminder referencing your initial request. Anchor your inquiry around objective competencies:\n\n> *\"Dear [HR / Hiring Manager],*  \n>  \n> *I hope you are well. I am following up on my request from [Date] regarding written feedback following my final-round interviews for the Head of Structural Engineering position.*  \n>  \n> *Given my 15 years of design practice, chartered status, and track record directing major projects, I would greatly appreciate specific insight into how the panel evaluated core competencies (such as technical leadership, business development, and team management) against the criteria for the role. This feedback will be valuable for my ongoing professional development.*  \n>  \n> *Thank you for your time, and I look forward to hearing from you.\"*\n\n#### **Step 2: Maintain a Contemporaneous Record**\nKeep a private log of:\n* The original job specification and required qualifications.\n* Your interview notes (questions asked, answers provided, any comments made by panel members).\n* Dates and copies of all correspondence.\n* The public announcement text regarding the appointment.\n\n#### **Step 3: Analyze the Feedback When Received**\nOnce they provide written feedback (or if they refuse to):\n* **Did the criteria shift?** If the job description emphasized technical oversight and major project delivery, but the decision rested almost exclusively on network/relationship factors not heavily weighted in the specification, that warrants attention.\n* **If you suspect systemic bias:** If the feedback is vague or reveals inconsistent standards, you can consult with an employment law specialist or your professional engineering association/union to review whether you have grounds for a formal grievance or information request under local employment regulations.\n\n#### **Step 4: Assess the Firm's Culture**\nA company whose senior leadership lacks gender diversity and fails to provide timely, transparent feedback after two rounds of senior-level interviews reveals how they operate. Regardless of the legal or formal outcome, consider whether this firm offers the structural support and leadership equity you would need to thrive in the long term.\n",
